member.summary:
A body defined by its half-width per row - a bottle, a vase, a rocket, a
tree trunk, a lighthouse. Instead of assembling primitives you describe the
silhouette once and then ask it what belongs to the shape.

This is what makes a hand-drawn object look designed rather than assembled:
the outline is authored row by row, and everything else (fill, label band,
highlight, glass) reads from the same profile.


var bottle = new ProfileShape(centerX: 12, rows: new (double Y, double HalfWidth)[]
{
    (2, 2.0), (4, 2.4), (6, 1.9), (9, 5.6), (12, 6.0), (21, 6.0), (22, 5.3),
});
bottle.Fill(ctx, (x, y, edge) => ColorOps.Lerp(glass, glassEdge, edge * edge));

Applies a rule to every cell simultaneously (double buffered, so a cell
never sees a half-updated neighbourhood).

life.Step((x, y, self, n) => n == 3 || (self != 0 && n == 2) ? (byte)1 : (byte)0);

Travels from A to B along an arc instead of a straight line.
 is how far the arc bows out, in pixels;
positive bows one way, negative the other.

A top-down map of a world for debugging: where objects really are, where an
actor is heading, and what the camera can see. On 24x24 this is the only
practical way to check navigation and collision logic, because the scene
view itself hides most of the world.

Draw it instead of the scene while debugging:

var map = new DebugMap(ctx, worldHalfWidth: 25, worldDepth: 30);
map.Disc(island.Wx, island.Wz, 2.0, Colors.Green);
map.Ring(island.Wx, island.Wz, 6.0, Colors.Yellow);
map.Frustum(camera, Colors.SlateGray);
map.Marker(boat.Wx, boat.Wz, Colors.Red, size: 2);


Deliberately the only block that binds ctx in its constructor - it is a
debug tool, not a scene building block.

A persistent buffer that keeps what was drawn in earlier frames and dims it
a little every frame - motion trails, phosphor glow, light painting, comet
tails, matrix rain.

The canvas itself is cleared before every frame, so a trail CANNOT be made
by simply not clearing. Declare the buffer OUTSIDE the scene lambda; it is
the only thing that survives.

Two ways to use it:

// A) paint INTO the trail: the buffer is the drawing surface
trails.Decay();
trails.Surface.DrawCircle(x, y, 2, colorFill: Colors.Cyan);
trails.Draw(ctx, BlendMode.Plus);

// B) echo the CANVAS: draw the scene normally, then feed it to the buffer
trails.Decay();
ctx.DrawSurface(trails.Surface, blendMode: BlendMode.Plus);
DrawScene(ctx);
trails.Capture(ctx, 0.6);


Warning: the internal  has frozen time (Elapsed 0, Now
at construction) - anything reading time from it silently gets constants;
take time from the outer ctx instead.

What changed between two states of a surface - the "what did I just
draw" mask.

var before = ctx.Snapshot();
DrawSubject(ctx);
var subject = Mask.FromDiff(before, ctx);
subject.DrawHalo(ctx, Colors.Gold, 2);

Sets a value from its canonical text form (as produced by
). Out-of-range numbers are clamped,
unknown names and unparsable values are rejected.

example.code:
var speed = Param.Float(1.0, min: 0.1, max: 5.0);
var count = Param.Int(5, min: 1, max: 20, label: "Star count");
var glow  = Param.Bool(true);
var tint  = Param.Color(Colors.Cyan);
var mood  = Param.Choice("calm", ["calm", "lively", "wild"]);

var scene = (RasterSurface ctx) =>
    ctx.DrawCircle(ctx.Elapsed.TotalSeconds * speed, 12, count, tint);

member.summary:
Verlet points plus distance constraints - ropes, chains, pendulums, flags
and soft bodies in a handful of lines, and far more readable on 24x24 than
exact rigid-body dynamics. Velocity is implicit (current minus previous
position), which makes the integration unconditionally stable.

Step it at a FIXED rate () - verlet assumes uniform
steps, a variable frame delta makes it breathe.


var rope = VerletSystem.Rope(4, 2, 4, 16, segments: 9, pinStart: true);
rope.Force = VectorField.Sum(VectorField.Constant(6, 0), VectorField.Curl());
rope.Constrain = p => (p.X, p.Y) = Geometry.PushOutOfDisc(p.X, p.Y, 14, 12, 3.5);
foreach (var _ in sim.Steps(ctx)) rope.Step(1.0 / 30, t);

A sprite written directly in the source as rows of characters, with a
character-to-colour table. No asset file, no loader, no path - the picture
IS the code, which is exactly right for the handful of small figures a
24x24 scene needs.


var dog = PixelSprite.FromRows(new[]
{
    ".bb..bb.",
    "bwwwwwwb",
    "bwkwwkwb",
    ".bwwwwb.",
}, new Dictionary<char, Color>
{
    ['b'] = Color.FromRgb(0.30, 0.18, 0.08),
    ['w'] = Color.FromRgb(0.85, 0.75, 0.55),
    ['k'] = Colors.Black,
});
dog.Draw(ctx, 8, 12);

Any character not in the table (by convention '.') is transparent.

A named sequence of timed segments - the dramaturgy of a scene in one place
instead of a stack of hand-summed constants.


var story = new Storyboard(loop: true, seamFade: 0.5)
    .Add("grow", 5.0).Add("fall", 2.4).Add("hold", 4.5);

var cue = story.At(ctx);
if (cue.Is("fall")) DrawFall(ctx, cue.Progress);

A simulation that runs at its OWN rate, independent of the frame rate. Game of Life at 40 steps per second is unreadable; a falling-sand pile that only moves when the frame rate allows is unpredictable. Both want a fixed step.
Declare it OUTSIDE the scene lambda.
var sim = new FixedStep(stepsPerSecond: 8); // outside foreach (var _ in sim.Steps(ctx)) grid.StepLife();

A hand-set bitmap font: glyphs written as rows of '#' and '.', drawn pixel
exact. When a scene needs three digits in a corner, or a wordmark that must
hit exact pixels, a rasterised TTF is the wrong tool - this is authored at
the pixel level and never surprises you.


var digits = PixelFont.FromRows(3, 5, new Dictionary<char, string[]>
{
    ['0'] = new[] { "###", "#.#", "#.#", "#.#", "###" },
    ['1'] = new[] { ".#.", "##.", ".#.", ".#.", "###" },
});
digits.Draw(ctx, "10", 2, 3, Colors.White);

A voxel model: a list of unit cubes plus a material table, parsed from the plain-text .vox format or built in code. The text format is the interesting part - it is small enough to write by hand (or by a model) inside a pixogram, and the same files drive the voxel tools in the pxl-clock repo.
var model = VoxModel.Parse(""" material sand top=#F4E0A0 front=#A89060 material palm top=#5A8E32 front=#365A1E box 0 0 0 6 0 6 sand # from..to inclusive, then material v 3 1 3 palm # a single voxel """);
Coordinates: x to the right, y UP from the base, z into the scene. Every voxel fills the unit cube from (x, y, z) to (x+1, y+1, z+1). Duplicate voxels at the same cell resolve last-definition-wins, so detail lines can override an earlier box fill.

Draws the model onto the surface, composited over whatever is there.
The model is centred on the canvas (plus camera pan); 
hooks the finished face colour, e.g. c => fog.Apply(c, wz, ...).
